{
  // Output filename and BoardLoader data-variable name:
  //   boardName "RP2350" -> designer/boards/RP2350/RP2350.json -> const RP2350Data = {...}
  "boardName": "RP2350",

  // Fallback human-readable name shown in the designer UI. Overridden by
  // the matching `pimoroni_pga2350.name=` entry in variants/rp2040/
  // boards.txt ("Pimoroni PGA2350") when present.
  "displayName": "Pimoroni PGA2350",

  // Same parser dialect as every other RP2040/RP2350 board — see
  // ../board.json for the full rationale.
  "family": "rp2040",

  // No genuinely bidirectional-but-input-only pins confirmed on this
  // board beyond the chip-level default.
  "inputOnlyGpios": [],

  // Default transports — Serial only. Bare RP2350B breakout module, no
  // onboard radio.
  "connections": ["serial"],

  "pinNotes": {},

  // pfodWeb NOTE (RP2040/RP2350 family audit, button/LED/NeoPixel
  // tagging + dedicated-hardware pin exclusion, 2026-07-13):
  //
  // Board: Pimoroni PGA2350 (PIM722), chip rp2350 (RP2350B, PICO_RP2350A
  // set to 0 in this board's own pins_arduino.h). This board's own
  // variants/rp2040/rp2350/pimoroni_pga2350/pins_arduino.h contains only:
  //   #define RP2350_PSRAM_CS    47
  //   #define PICO_RP2350A 0 // RP2350B
  //   #include "../generic/pins_arduino.h"
  // — i.e. it declares NO board-specific pin aliases of its own at all
  // (no PIN_LED, no PIN_BUTTON, no PIN_NEOPIXEL), falling back entirely
  // to the shared variants/rp2040/rp2350/generic/pins_arduino.h +
  // generic/common.h fallback (same mechanism used by the sibling
  // pimoroni_pga2040 board in the rp2040 chip bucket, which likewise has
  // no board.json and no board-specific pins_arduino.h aliases).
  //
  // Sources consulted (WebSearch/WebFetch, 2026-07-13):
  //  1. shop.pimoroni.com/en-us/products/pga2350 (official Pimoroni
  //     product page, fetched directly) — describes the PGA2350 as "a
  //     minimal but powerful RP2350 breakout board modelled on a Pin
  //     Grid Array, with the maximum exposed pins crammed into the
  //     smallest possible space," built around the RP2350B (dual Arm
  //     Cortex-M33, up to 150MHz, 520KB SRAM), with onboard 8MB PSRAM
  //     and 16MB flash, exposing 64 pins at 2.54mm pitch (48 usable as
  //     general-purpose I/O, 8 ADC-equipped), 25.4mm square footprint.
  //     States VERBATIM: "Note that it has no LEDs, buttons or USB
  //     connectors - you'll need to attach your own USB connector to be
  //     able to program it." Header pins are sold separately
  //     (shop.pimoroni.com/en-us/products/header-pins-and-sockets-for-
  //     pga2040-style boards) — this board ships as a bare module.
  //  2. WebSearch cross-references (cpc.farnell.com/pimoroni/pim722,
  //     pishop.us/product/pga2350, pishop.ca/product/pga2350,
  //     wholesale.pimoroni.com/en-us/products/pga2350) all independently
  //     repeat the same "no LEDs, buttons or USB connectors" feature
  //     description sourced from Pimoroni's own product copy.
  //  3. variants/rp2040/boards.txt: `pimoroni_pga2350.build.led=` is
  //     declared but left EMPTY (no GPIO number assigned) — consistent
  //     with "no onboard LED" at the boards.txt/build-flag level too
  //     (same empty-build.led pattern as the sibling pimoroni_pga2040
  //     entry, which is confirmed by Pimoroni's own PGA2040 product page
  //     to likewise have "no fripperies like LEDs, buttons and USB
  //     connectors").
  //
  // Per-pin findings:
  //   No onboard LED, NeoPixel, or button of any kind exists on this
  //   board — confirmed directly from Pimoroni's own product page (see
  //   source #1 above), corroborated by boards.txt's empty build.led.
  //   NO CHANGE: no chipGpios entries added for any button/LED/NeoPixel
  //   tag, matching the confirmed absence of such hardware.
  //
  //   GPIO47 (RP2350_PSRAM_CS) — onboard 8MB PSRAM chip-select,
  //   confirmed via this board's own pins_arduino.h (`RP2350_PSRAM_CS
  //   47`) and the vendor-documented "8MB of PSRAM" spec (source #1).
  //   Same situation as variants/rp2040/rp2350/pimoroni_pico_plus_2/
  //   board.json's GPIO47 finding: this pin needs NO chipGpios override
  //   here either. It is declared only as a bare `#define`, with no
  //   `static const uint8_t` peer alias anywhere in this board's
  //   pins_arduino.h or the shared generic/pins_arduino.h +
  //   generic/common.h it falls back to (verified — generic/common.h's
  //   only static-const aliases are D0-D22, A0-A2, and
  //   SS/MOSI/MISO/SCK/SDA/SCL, none aliasing GPIO47). build_boards.js
  //   only adds a GPIO to the routable pin set from (a) a resolved
  //   `static const uint8_t` alias, or (b) an explicit chipGpios key —
  //   GPIO47 satisfies neither, so it is already correctly absent from
  //   the generated pin list with no board.json change needed. Adding a
  //   chipGpios entry for it would be counter-productive: chipGpios keys
  //   are unconditionally added to the routable-pin set, so an
  //   empty-capabilities override would introduce a previously-absent
  //   inert "GPIO47" pin into the Designer where none is generated
  //   today — the opposite of the desired exclusion. Left untouched.
  //
  //   PIN_LED (GPIO25, via generic/pins_arduino.h's unconditional
  //   `#define PIN_LED (25u)`) — also NOT swept into the routable pin
  //   set for the same reason as GPIO47: generic/common.h's
  //   `#define LED_BUILTIN PIN_LED` is a plain macro, not a `static
  //   const uint8_t` alias, so it never resolves to a chipGpios-visible
  //   GPIO here. This is actually correct for THIS board regardless: the
  //   generic fallback's PIN_LED=25 reflects the Raspberry Pi Pico
  //   reference design's onboard-LED convention, not anything specific
  //   to the PGA2350 hardware, which vendor sources confirm has no LED
  //   at all. No action needed/possible either way.
  //
  //   GPIO0-22, 26-28 (D0-D22, A0-A2 via generic/common.h) — the only
  //   GPIOs actually swept into this board's routable pin set by the
  //   generic fallback's static-const aliases. These are genuinely
  //   general-purpose header pins on the real PGA2350 (which exposes 48
  //   of its 64 pins as GPIO per source #1) — left at chip-level default
  //   (digital_input/digital_output/pwm_output, no button/LED tag),
  //   which is correct since none of them are confirmed to be
  //   button/LED/NeoPixel pins. Note the generic fallback's 26-pin cap
  //   (PINS_COUNT=26) significantly under-represents the real board's
  //   48-GPIO count — this is a pre-existing scope-of-fallback
  //   limitation (see generic/common.h's own pfodWeb NOTE on GPIO23/24/
  //   25/29 assuming a Pico-shaped reference design), not a button/LED/
  //   NeoPixel tagging or dedicated-hardware-pin-exclusion issue, and is
  //   therefore out of scope for this audit — flagged here for
  //   visibility only, not acted on. Expanding pin coverage would require
  //   a genuinely custom pins_arduino.h for this board (bypassing the
  //   generic fallback entirely), which is a separate, larger change.
  //
  // No SDA/SCL/SS/MOSI/MISO/SCK bus-collision bug applies: there is no
  // confirmed button/LED/NeoPixel pin on this board at all (see above),
  // so build_boards.js's unconditional SS/SDA/SCL/MOSI/MISO/SCK alias
  // auto-append cannot collide with anything here.
  //
  // Full findings/sources trail:
  // boardsDetails/rp2040/rp2350/pimoroni_pga2350/notes.txt
  "chipGpios": {},

  // Same 8-bit analogWrite() default as every other RP2040/RP2350 board.
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },

  // No hardware DAC on RP2040/RP2350.
  "dac": {},

  // RP2350's 12-bit ADC (0-4095), ~3.3V reference — same as every other
  // plain RP2040/RP2350 board using the generic fallback (this board's
  // own pins_arduino.h declares no custom analog pins).
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" }
}
